  "worked_solution": "The function is strictly decreasing where ${g}^{\u2032}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{\u2032}(x) & =12{x}^{3}-24{x}^{2}+12x \\\\  & =12x({x}^{2}-2x+1) \\\\  & =12x{(x-1)}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n12x{(x-1)}^{2}<0.\n$$\n\nSince $(x-1)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all real $x$, the sign of $12x{(x-1)}^{2}$ is determined by the sign of $12x$, so $12x{(x-1)}^{2}<0$ exactly when $12x<0.$ This yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x & <0 \\\\ x & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is restricted to $x\\in (-8,8),$ we conclude that $g(x)$ is strictly decreasing on the interval $x\\in (-8,0).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
